WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.350
- Narrator: What makes
a great superhero movie?

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.200
It's the million dollar
question in Hollywood right now,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.690
or more accurately six billion,

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.450
with Marvel creating what
is now the most successful

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:18.980
film franchise in film history
based solely on superheros.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.350
And after decades of this superhero craze,

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.630
we've essentially seen
every possible iterations

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.230
or versions of the genre.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.490
- I'm Batman.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.900
Some are excellent, pushing boundaries,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.190
and attempting to redefine the genre.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.357
- Oh so this is what it feels like.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.430
- Narrator: While others, well...

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.920
Marvel and DC movies can
be fun and enjoyable,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.298
but there's one film
that really exemplifies

00:00:50.298 --> 00:00:52.610
a great superhero movie.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.930
Let's go all the way back to 1999,

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.279
the year Wachowskis released
their cyberpunk masterpiece.

00:00:58.279 --> 00:00:59.300
- Freeze!

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.500
- Narrator: The Matrix.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.060
Despite its heavy sci-fi undertones,

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.350
The Matrix, at its core,
is a superhero movie.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.220
I mean look at it.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.930
An unlikely hero develops unique powers

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.400
to protect the world from evil.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.580
Now add an iconic look, a
few memorable set pieces,

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.140
and this.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.780
That's right, Marvel
and DC have The Matrix

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.150
to thank for this iconic
three-point hero landing

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.950
that has become the staple of the genre.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.640
Put all of this together and
you've got the classic formula

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.193
for a superhero film.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.980
But what makes The Matrix shine

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.030
is in its narrative structure,

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.250
specifically the journey
the Keanu Reaves' character

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.850
Thomas Anderson goes through
to become Neo, the chosen one.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.470
To better understand
this, let's take a look

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.890
at a narrative structure
called the hero's journey,

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.470
by Joseph Campbell.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.620
Campbell believed that
every piece of fiction

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.240
regarding a hero goes through
a very specific set of stages,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.620
17, to be precise.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.430
Later screenwriter Christopher Vogler

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:04.940
would simplify these 17 stages down to 12.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.650
These stages then can be broken
into three distinct acts,

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.903
departure, initiation, and return.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.780
Every film about a hero
follows this distinct pattern.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.870
The hero sets on a journey,
faces a series of challenges,

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:24.380
and then returns home
after a triumphant victory.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.530
Most superhero blockbusters
focus on this second act,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.910
the initiation, often characterized

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.043
by these big final boss battles.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.320
It's what also makes these
films so entertaining to watch,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.060
as everything builds up to
one final, climactic battle,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.300
that Vogler names The Ordeal,

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.623
where our hero faces the
most difficult challenge yet.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.870
But what happens afterwards?

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.793
Is this part of the movie
as memorable to you?

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:57.940
Probably not.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.960
Which is a shame, because
this final act, the return,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.960
is perhaps the most important
part of any hero's journey.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:09.453
And this is where The Matrix truly shines.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.460
Let's step back and look at The Ordeal.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.710
It occurs in The Matrix
when Neo and Trinity

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.420
set out on an impossible mission

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.900
to rescue Morpheus from the
clutches of the evil agents.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.000
Our heroes are successful and for a second

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.000
it seems as though things
are starting to wrap up.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.910
But that's not quite what happens here.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.530
As they return to the
real world one by one,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.170
they are compromised and Neo is left

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.603
to face the agents alone
for the first time.

00:03:36.603 --> 00:03:37.611
- Neo.

00:03:37.611 --> 00:03:38.730
- What the hell just happened?

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.820
- Narrator: This stage is
referred to as the road back

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.017
by Vogler where the hero

00:03:43.017 --> 00:03:45.327
"begins to deal with the consequences

00:03:45.327 --> 00:03:48.267
"of confronting the dark
forces of The Ordeal

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:52.350
"and is pursued by the vengeful
forces he has disturbed."

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:53.840
That's exactly what happens,

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.050
a chase between Neo and the agents

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.270
as he searches for a way back.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:03:59.780
Neo is eventually cornered,

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.132
and then something truly unique happens.

00:04:04.132 --> 00:04:06.940
(gun fires)

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.430
This scene came as a shock
to a lot of the people

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.910
watching The Matrix for the first time

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:17.180
and it's a shock that is well
deserved by the Wachowskis.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.640
The film is riddled with uncertainties

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.182
about whether Thomas is truly Neo,

00:04:22.182 --> 00:04:24.430
the prophesized chosen one.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.800
At one point we even hear that he isn't,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.280
from the prophet herself.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.117
- You already know what
I'm going to tell you.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.290
- I'm not the one.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.840
- Sorry kid.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.310
- Narrator: Ironically, and symbolically,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.650
the person who has the
most trouble beleiveing

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.370
is none other than Thomas himself.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.010
This is beautifully
visualized when Neo fails

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.810
to make a seemingly impossible jump,

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.230
a metaphorical leap of faith.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.290
- You have to let it all go Neo.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.723
Fear, doubt, and disbelief.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.503
Free your mind.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.170
- Narrator: And that's why perhaps

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:05.250
the best cinematic moment in The Matrix

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.353
happens in this scene.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.093
- Mr. Anderson.

00:05:12.996 --> 00:05:14.246
- Run Neo, run.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.370
- Narrator: How do you visualize faith?

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.740
To the Wachowskis, that answer is choice.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.460
Upon further viewing, it's surprising

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.700
to see just how many times choices

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.950
are visualized throughout the film.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:35.130
But it asks the same question every time.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.880
Do you choose to escape and avoid

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.410
the harsh reality of truth,

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:42.750
or do you choose to
confront and believe it.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.860
When all odds are against Thomas,

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.380
he chooses to believe in himself.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.330
- What is he doing?

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.530
- He's beginning to believe.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.180
- Narrator: That's when we
witness a transformation,

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:58.110
a moment in which a
simple man becomes a hero.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.570
And when our faith for the
hero is finally proven right,

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.683
it feels well deserved.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.540
This stage, by the way, is
actually called resurrection,

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:10.277
where the hero is
"purified and transformed

00:06:10.277 --> 00:06:13.470
"through the most dangerous
meeting with death."

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.500
And perhaps this is where a lot

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.430
of superhero films today fall short.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:21.590
Most of them choose to
follow a safe, proven formula

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:25.740
that neither raises the stakes,
nor truly tests our hero.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:26.573
- Anyone else?

00:06:26.573 --> 00:06:28.900
- Narrator: We're supposed to
simply accept them as heroes

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.680
without knowing why it
is that they deserve it.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.170
It makes for an easy viewing,

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.880
but also a highly predictable one.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.650
There are of course a few exceptions,

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.180
most notably in films
like The Dark Knight,

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.290
or recently, Black Panther.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.810
But it seems that many
still fail to understand

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.100
why it is that we love
superheroes so much.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.510
As much as we love watching
our heroes fight and win,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.190
it's the side of them
that makes them human,

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.380
their vulnerability, but
still the willingness to fight

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.860
against all odds, that
keeps us coming back.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.510
Because it's only after
moments like these,

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.723
after a truly harrowing journey,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.273
that a real hero is born.

